\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces A workflow net with a silent transition. Circles are places, white boxes visible transitions, and the black box a silent transition ${"τ}$. The token in the source place $i$ is a truck before the gate. The silent transition skips the yard stop, so the net also accepts a visit without any stop.}}{10}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces A real ProSiT tree: the arrival model learned from the CTB training partition (ProSiT 1.0.4, Section~\ref {sec:exp_baseline}). The tree predicts the gap until the next truck arrives, in open minutes of the arrival calendar, from the hour and weekday of the previous arrival. Each leaf holds a fitted distribution. The simulator follows the conditions from the top to one leaf and draws the gap from that leaf. The means are those of the values the simulator draws. Sunday is closed in the arrival calendar, so no gaps are drawn there.}}{21}{figure.caption.13}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Steps of one simulated visit in the final model and where each value comes from. Blue: learned by ProSiT from the training log. Orange border: adaptation to the terminal (Section~\ref {sec:sim_adaptations}). Grey: structure and output.}}{36}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Control flow of the simulation model: IM 0.0 restricted to rules 3 and 5 as a state machine (10 places, 54 transitions, no silent transitions). The labels show the stop guard of the simulator: $n$ is the number of planned stops from the manifest and $c$ the number of stops done; a yard transition may fire only if $c < n$, Gate Out only if $c = n$.}}{37}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Arrival tree: minutes to the next Gate In by weekday (0 = Monday) and hour of the last arrival. Each leaf shows the training values (bars) and the fitted distribution (line), each on its own scale.}}{40}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Routing tree of the transition \texttt {RMG\_receive} at the first decision after Gate In: probability that it fires when enabled, by manifest attributes.}}{40}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces First stop of a visit by flow type: share in the training log and probability under the learned routing (normalised weights of the enabled transitions, averaged over 8,000 training manifests).}}{41}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Waiting-time tree of block T13 (final model): minutes from a free lane to the start of the stop. All leaves on one scale.}}{41}{figure.caption.25}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.7}{\ignorespaces Execution-time tree of RMG receives (final model). The first splits are on the number of other trucks at the block when the stop starts.}}{42}{figure.caption.26}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Flow of the experiments E1 to E3. Each stage reduces the set of candidate models and passes it on. E4 and E5 continue with the configuration selected in E3.}}{46}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Repair pattern for rule~5: one exclusive choice across two parts of the net. The first choice leaves a token that records the decision (dashed places and arcs). \texttt {LL\_delivery} can only fire if \texttt {LL\_mixed} was skipped. The skip of the second choice exists twice, once for each token, so no control token remains at the end. \texttt {LL\_receive} is connected like \texttt {LL\_delivery}, and HO2 is repaired in the same way. Per handling area, the pattern adds two places and one silent transition.}}{57}{figure.caption.36}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces Marker logic for rule~3 (at least one stop), shown for two optional stops in parallel. Gate In puts a token into \emph {no stop yet}. Each stop exists twice: the first stop moves the token to \emph {stop done}, a further stop only reads it (double arrow). Gate Out needs \emph {stop done}, so a visit cannot leave without a stop. If both stops are skipped, however, the token stays in \emph {no stop yet} and the visit can never finish. The complete construction therefore also forbids skipping the last open stop, with one more control place per stop (not shown).}}{57}{figure.caption.37}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Share of fitting visits and precision of the discovered nets on the training log, which is the basis for the selection. Left: all nets. Right: the nets with more than 91\,\% fitting visits and a precision above 0.7. Hollow markers: unsound nets, values for diagnosis only. The prefix tree accepts exactly the training sequences and serves as a reference.}}{73}{figure.caption.44}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces The same nets on the test log (13 to 30 April 2026). The test log was not used for any decision. Precision hardly changes. IM 0.0 and ILP 1.00 still fit (almost) all visits, while the nets that accept few sequences lose more fitting visits than on the training log (IMf 0.3: 96.5\,\% $\rightarrow $ 94.9\,\%).}}{73}{figure.caption.45}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.3}{\ignorespaces Which training sequences each sound candidate accepts. Top: share of the training visits per sequence (logarithmic scale), from the most to the least frequent sequence. Middle: number of stops of each sequence. Bottom: one row per net; a coloured cell means that the net accepts the complete sequence. The six most frequent sequences cover 88.5\,\% of the visits, so a net that accepts few sequences can still fit most visits.}}{75}{figure.caption.46}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.4}{\ignorespaces Fitness--precision trade-off of the discovered nets on the validation partition. Left: all nets with an alignment; the dashed rectangle marks the zoomed region. Right: the candidates with more than 91\,\% fitting visits. Boxes summarise soundness, size and the coverage measures of Table~\ref {tab:res_coverage}. Hollow grey markers: the unsound Alpha nets. The prefix tree is a reference, not a candidate.}}{79}{figure.caption.51}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.5}{\ignorespaces Selected net IM 0.0 (Inductive Miner without filtering, discovered in ProM on the full training partition): 28 places, 11 visible and 27 silent transitions (black). The silent AND-split after \texttt {Gate In} lets the branches occur in any order. Every branch can be skipped.}}{81}{figure.caption.53}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.6}{\ignorespaces Repaired control-flow model \texttt {IM0\_R5\_R12} as a state machine (10 places, 54 transitions). Each place is a state of the visit. Repeated labels belong to different states.}}{86}{figure.caption.58}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.7}{\ignorespaces Turnaround time, observed and simulated (final model; replayed visits and observed blocks; five seeds pooled).}}{104}{figure.caption.68}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.8}{\ignorespaces Median turnaround time per Gate-In hour over the test period, observed and simulated with the same trucks (seed 42).}}{104}{figure.caption.69}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.9}{\ignorespaces Load profiles, observed and simulated, in the test period (solid) and the training period (dotted); medians, means over five seeds. With three lanes, the states ``4+ trucks at the block'' and ``3+ other trucks'' cannot occur in the simulation.}}{105}{figure.caption.70}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.10}{\ignorespaces Where the time of a visit is spent, observed and simulated (means).}}{105}{figure.caption.71}%
